[Music]  foreign  [Music]  for Thursday December 8th I'm Zoe Thomas  for The Wall Street Journal  a wsj exclusive we report a potential  deal between Tick Tock and the Biden  Administration has run into delays  that's according to people familiar with  the situation the deal aims to address  National Security concerns that U.S  officials say the app poses because of  its owner the beijing-based tech giant  bite dance officials said those ties  could give China an in to collect data  on U.S users a potential deal has been  in the works for more than a year one  was expected by the end of this year but  now it's run into further delays so  what's the hold up and what would it  mean for tick tock and its users in the  U.S if negotiations drag on  John McKinnon reports on regulatory  issues from Washington and he's with us  now hi John thanks for joining me nice  to be with you let's start with a bit of  backstory how did we get to this point  where the U.S and Tick Tock are  discussing a deal well these discussions  have actually been going on for quite a  while late in the Trump Administration  there was just a growing concern about  the possible security risks posed by  Tick Tock and some people will remember  that the U.S military has at that point  blocked service people from using Tick  Tock and there have been a few other  changes along the way in the  relationship between the U.S and Tick  Tock and so the U.S and Tick Tock  started negotiating over some sort of  deal to sort of ensure that our national  security interests were going to be  protected okay but now they're running  into delays in these conversations so  what are some of the sticking points  there are probably many sticking points  but I would say that the two biggest  ones revolve around how U.S users data  is going to be protected from the  potential prying eyes of the Chinese  government and then the other one has to  do with the algorithm that recommends  videos to users there is concern among  U.S officials that the algorithm could  be manipulated to help an influence  operation that the Chinese government  wanted to engage in in the U.S or spread  misinformation or disinformation along  the lines of what happened in 2016  during the presidential election those  are issues that were raised actually by  the FBI director Christopher Wray in  some testimony that he gave to Congress  a few weeks back so we know that the  government itself has these concerns  about their relationship with Tick Tock  now Tick Tock has always maintained that  it wouldn't share U.S data with  Beijing is still saying that it's  optimistic essentially that some  agreement is going to be reached and  they say that they are expecting a  timely conclusion to their negotiations  with the government and they point out  that they've already actually started  implementing some of the changes that  they're expecting to be in the agreement  such as you know uh Stronger barriers  essentially to protect the U.S users  data I want to talk a bit about the  risks if this drags on too long I mean  are there concerns for both sides if the  negotiations keep getting delayed there  are certainly concerns that this  agreement is going to be difficult to  work out if the U.S security officials  if people at the justice department and  people in the FBI are continuing to  raise objections to the draft agreement  that was worked out earlier this year  there's just concern at least among some  people that Tick Tock its owners and the  Chinese are simply not going to be  willing to go a lot further and so then  you have a situation where the U.S faces  a couple of pretty unpleasant options  basically what are those unpleasant  options well the ultimate unpleasant  option I think politically would be a  ban on Tick Tock and the Trump  Administration threatened to do that  back in 2020 but never managed to pull  it off and you know there's certainly  widespread concern that there would be a  backlash a popular backlash if the  government actually tried to impose some  kind of ban on Tick Tock the other  option which is maybe almost as  unpalatable is for the administration  to do nothing and expose itself to a lot  of criticism especially from republicans  in Congress that they're not doing  enough or not doing anything really to  try to protect the U.S from the risks  that are seen with Tick Tock what's the  role of the Chinese government in these  negotiations I mean does it have one the  Chinese government has not had a lot to  say about these negotiations during the  Trump Administration they were pretty  clearly concerned about the possibility  that the U.S or its Representatives  would gain access to the algorithm that  Tick Tock uses which is based on an  algorithm that they developed for China  the Chinese government essentially said  none of these algorithms that are the  intellectual property of Chinese  companies can be exported I would say  that's the big signal they've sent that  they really want this to remain  essentially a Chinese Enterprise  and they haven't really had a lot to say  lately about the National Security  agreement negotiations but I will say  that Tick Tock I think rightly so is  concerned that if these negotiations go  too far in  reducing the influence of Chinese owners  and the Chinese government than the  Chinese government might ultimately  reject the deal all right that's a  reporter John McKinnon John thanks so  much for joining us you're welcome and  that's it for today's Tech news briefing  if you want more Tech stories head over  to our website wsj.com I'm Zoe Thomas  for The Wall Street Journal thanks for  listening  [Music]  foreign

